{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Taskmasters over them, to afflict them</b> — He imposed a levy upon the people, to take men from them for the king's labor, and he appointed officers over the levy from among the Egyptians, who would take men from them at will according to the labor — in shifts, a month or more they would serve in the king's construction, and the rest of the days in their own homes. And these officers commanded them to build cities for Pharaoh. <b>And the people built</b> — the people <b>built store-cities for Pharaoh</b> under this levy. And when they saw that this did not harm the people, they loathed their lives because of them, and they decreed that all Egypt should enslave the people: any Egyptian who needed labor would have authority to take men from them to do his work. And this is the meaning of <b>And the Egyptians made the children of Israel serve with rigor</b>. <b>And further</b>, they decreed hard labors upon them <b>in mortar and in bricks</b>: for at first the officers would supply them with bricks, and the men of the levy would do the construction; but now the people were subjected to labor, and they were commanded to bring earth and make the mortar with their hands and feet, and nothing would be given to them from the king's house except straw alone, and they would hand the bricks to the men of the levy who built the structure. And also <b>all hard labor</b> belonging to Pharaoh and to Egypt <b>in the field</b> — such as digging and hauling dung — all of it they imposed upon them. And they would also drive them harshly to press them so they would have no rest, striking them and cursing them. And this is the meaning of <b>all their labor wherein they made them serve with rigor</b>. <b>And</b> the king would provide them with meager bread, as is the custom for the king's laborers; and this is the statement of those who craved, who said, \"We remember the fish which we ate in Egypt for nothing, the cucumbers\" (Numbers 11:5) — for fish in Egypt was very abundant, and they would take them from the riverbanks by order of the king, and take cucumbers and melons from the gardens, and no one would object, for it was by order of the king. And our Sages said: they were slaves to kings and not slaves to slaves (Mekhilta, BeChodesh, end of chapter 5). If so, <b>And the Egyptians served</b> alludes to the taskmasters of the levy who belonged to Pharaoh."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:16:21.554447879Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}